WEBVTT

00:00.590 --> 00:02.540
When we're dealing
with basic arithmetic,

00:02.540 --> 00:04.770
we see the concrete
numbers there.

00:04.770 --> 00:07.262
We'll see 23 plus 5.

00:07.262 --> 00:09.220
We know what these numbers
are right over here,

00:09.220 --> 00:10.303
and we can calculate them.

00:10.303 --> 00:11.610
It's going to be 28.

00:11.610 --> 00:13.920
We can say 2 times 7.

00:13.920 --> 00:17.510
We could say 3 divided by 4.

00:17.510 --> 00:19.940
In all of these cases, we
know exactly what numbers

00:19.940 --> 00:20.930
we're dealing with.

00:20.930 --> 00:23.791
As we start entering into
the algebraic world--

00:23.791 --> 00:26.040
and you probably have seen
this a little bit already--

00:26.040 --> 00:30.080
we start dealing with
the ideas of variables.

00:30.080 --> 00:31.820
And variables, there's
a bunch of ways

00:31.820 --> 00:33.653
you can think about
them, but they're really

00:33.653 --> 00:36.240
just values in expressions
where they can change.

00:36.240 --> 00:38.550
The values in those
expressions can change.

00:38.550 --> 00:44.770
For example, if
I write x plus 5,

00:44.770 --> 00:46.760
this is an expression
right over here.

00:46.760 --> 00:49.110
This can take on
some value depending

00:49.110 --> 00:51.630
on what the value of x is.

00:51.630 --> 01:00.450
If x is equal to
1, then x plus 5,

01:00.450 --> 01:05.870
our expression right over here,
is going to be equal to 1.

01:05.870 --> 01:07.110
Because now x is 1.

01:07.110 --> 01:11.050
It'll be 1 plus 5, so x
plus 5 will be equal to 6.

01:11.050 --> 01:20.330
If x is equal to, I don't
know, negative 7, then x plus 5

01:20.330 --> 01:25.020
is going to be equal to--
well, now x is negative 7.

01:25.020 --> 01:28.730
It's going to be negative 7
plus 5, which is negative 2.

01:28.730 --> 01:34.970
So notice x here is a
variable, and its value can

01:34.970 --> 01:37.840
change depending on the context.

01:37.840 --> 01:40.059
And this is in the
context of an expression.

01:40.059 --> 01:42.100
You'll also see it in the
context of an equation.

01:42.100 --> 01:43.660
It's actually
important to realize

01:43.660 --> 01:46.870
the distinction between an
expression and an equation.

01:46.870 --> 01:48.890
An expression is
really just a statement

01:48.890 --> 01:51.840
of value, a statement of
some type of quantity.

01:51.840 --> 01:54.290
So this is an expression.

01:54.290 --> 01:56.750
An expression would
be something like what

01:56.750 --> 01:59.220
we saw over here, x plus 5.

01:59.220 --> 02:00.700
The value of this
expression will

02:00.700 --> 02:05.740
change depending on what the
value of this variable is.

02:05.740 --> 02:09.050
And you could just evaluate
it for different values of x.

02:09.050 --> 02:11.770
Another expression could be
something like, I don't know,

02:11.770 --> 02:13.130
y plus z.

02:13.130 --> 02:14.430
Now everything is a variable.

02:14.430 --> 02:18.570
If y is 1 and z is 2,
it's going to be 1 plus 2.

02:18.570 --> 02:21.820
If y is 0 and z is
negative 1, it's

02:21.820 --> 02:24.476
going to be 0 plus negative 1.

02:24.476 --> 02:26.600
These can all be evaluated,
and they'll essentially

02:26.600 --> 02:29.820
give you a value depending
on the values of each

02:29.820 --> 02:32.226
of these variables that
make up the expression.

02:32.226 --> 02:34.350
An equation, you're
essentially setting expressions

02:34.350 --> 02:35.610
to be equal to each other.

02:35.610 --> 02:38.100
That's why they're
called equations.

02:38.100 --> 02:40.100
You're equating two things.

02:40.100 --> 02:43.060
An equation, you'll
see one expression

02:43.060 --> 02:44.830
being equal to
another expression.

02:44.830 --> 02:49.900
For example, you could say
something like x plus 3

02:49.900 --> 02:52.230
is equal to 1.

02:52.230 --> 02:57.250
And in this situation where you
have one equation with only one

02:57.250 --> 02:59.400
unknown, you can
actually figure out

02:59.400 --> 03:01.587
what x needs to be
in this scenario.

03:01.587 --> 03:03.170
And you might even
do it in your head.

03:03.170 --> 03:04.939
What plus 3 is equal to 1?

03:04.939 --> 03:06.480
Well, you could do
that in your head.

03:06.480 --> 03:09.220
If I have negative 2,
plus 3 is equal to 1.

03:09.220 --> 03:11.140
In this context, an
equation is starting

03:11.140 --> 03:14.892
to constrain what value
this variable can take on.

03:14.892 --> 03:16.850
But it doesn't have to
necessarily constrain it

03:16.850 --> 03:17.400
as much.

03:17.400 --> 03:25.750
You could have something like
x plus y plus z is equal to 5.

03:25.750 --> 03:29.360
Now you have this expression is
equal to this other expression.

03:29.360 --> 03:31.669
5 is really just an
expression right over here.

03:31.669 --> 03:32.960
And there are some constraints.

03:32.960 --> 03:35.220
If someone tells you
what y and z is, then

03:35.220 --> 03:36.480
you're going to get an x.

03:36.480 --> 03:38.250
If someone tells
you what x and y is,

03:38.250 --> 03:39.820
then that constrains what z is.

03:39.820 --> 03:42.660
But it depends on what
the different things are.

03:42.660 --> 03:51.610
For example, if we said y is
equal to 3 and z is equal to 2,

03:51.610 --> 03:53.600
then what would be
x in that situation?

03:53.600 --> 03:57.880
If y is equal to 3
and z is equal to 2,

03:57.880 --> 04:00.260
then you're going to have
the left-hand expression

04:00.260 --> 04:02.330
is going to be x plus 3 plus 2.

04:02.330 --> 04:05.030
It's going to be x plus 5.

04:05.030 --> 04:07.560
This part right over here is
just going to be 5. x plus 5

04:07.560 --> 04:08.920
is equal to 5.

04:08.920 --> 04:11.200
And so what plus
5 is equal to 5?

04:11.200 --> 04:14.750
Well, now we're
constraining that x

04:14.750 --> 04:18.269
would have to be equal to 0.

04:18.269 --> 04:19.769
Hopefully you realize
the difference

04:19.769 --> 04:20.899
between expression and equation.

04:20.899 --> 04:22.315
In an equation,
essentially you're

04:22.315 --> 04:23.640
equating two expressions.

04:23.640 --> 04:25.390
The important thing
to take away from here

04:25.390 --> 04:28.370
is that a variable can
take on different values

04:28.370 --> 04:31.570
depending on the
context of the problem.

04:31.570 --> 04:34.440
And to hit the point
home, let's just evaluate

04:34.440 --> 04:37.060
a bunch of expressions
when the variables have

04:37.060 --> 04:38.350
different values.

04:38.350 --> 04:47.890
For example, if we had the
expression x to the y power,

04:47.890 --> 04:55.930
if x is equal to 5
and y is equal to 2,

04:55.930 --> 04:59.060
then our expression here
is going to evaluate to,

04:59.060 --> 05:02.680
well, x is now going to be 5.

05:02.680 --> 05:04.400
y is going to be 2.

05:04.400 --> 05:06.630
It's going to be 5
to the second power,

05:06.630 --> 05:09.830
or it's going to evaluate to 25.

05:09.830 --> 05:16.250
If we changed the values-- let
me do that in that same color--

05:16.250 --> 05:24.850
if we said x is equal to
negative 2 and y is equal to 3,

05:24.850 --> 05:26.650
then this expression
would evaluate

05:26.650 --> 05:33.359
to-- let me do it in
that-- negative 2.

05:33.359 --> 05:34.900
That's what we're
going to substitute

05:34.900 --> 05:36.760
for x now in this context.

05:36.760 --> 05:42.300
And y is now 3, negative
2 to the third power,

05:42.300 --> 05:44.860
which is negative 2 times
negative 2 times negative 2,

05:44.860 --> 05:46.842
which is negative 8.

05:46.842 --> 05:49.300
Negative 2 times negative 2 is
positive 4, times negative 2

05:49.300 --> 05:51.670
again is equal to negative 8.

05:56.400 --> 05:58.310
We could do even
more complex things.

05:58.310 --> 06:03.290
We could have an expression
like the square root of x plus y

06:03.290 --> 06:06.600
and then minus x, like that.

06:09.310 --> 06:15.890
Let's say that x is equal
to 1 and y is equal to 8.

06:15.890 --> 06:18.920
Then this expression
would evaluate to, well,

06:18.920 --> 06:21.460
every time we see an x,
we want to put a 1 there.

06:21.460 --> 06:24.800
So we would have a 1 there,
and you'd have a 1 over there.

06:24.800 --> 06:28.350
And every time you see a y,
you would put an 8 in its place

06:28.350 --> 06:29.320
in this context.

06:29.320 --> 06:30.760
We're setting these variables.

06:30.760 --> 06:32.150
So you'd see an 8.

06:32.150 --> 06:34.640
Under the radical sign,
you would have a 1 plus 8.

06:34.640 --> 06:38.130
So you'd have the principal
root of 9, which is 3.

06:38.130 --> 06:39.970
This whole thing would simplify.

06:39.970 --> 06:41.850
In this context, when
we set these variables

06:41.850 --> 06:45.580
to be these things, this whole
thing would simplify to be 3.

06:45.580 --> 06:46.640
1 plus 8 is 9.

06:46.640 --> 06:48.740
The principal root of that is 3.

06:48.740 --> 06:54.490
And then you'd have 3 minus
1, which is equal to 2.
